{
  "concept": "The plenoptic function L(x, y, θ) assigns a light intensity to every direction at every point in space; sampling it at a point in free space gives a dense set of rays from all directions, while sampling it inside a pinhole camera gives an almost entirely zero function with energy only along the few directions the aperture admits.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "White scene background with a thick black ground line along the bottom",
    "Cartoon tree on the right: bright green lobed canopy, brown forked trunk with two small feet",
    "Small black outlined bird glyph in the upper right sky",
    "Pinhole camera on the left drawn as a tall open rectangle (black outline), labelled 'Pinhole camera' above it",
    "Pinhole aperture: small gap on the right-hand wall of the camera rectangle (the camera's front face)",
    "Two red sample points inside the camera on its left (image-plane) wall, upper and lower",
    "Two long arrows entering through the aperture and crossing, each terminating at one of the interior red points (upper scene ray → lower point, lower scene ray → upper point), showing image inversion",
    "Two red sample points in free space (mid-scene): one higher/right, one lower/left",
    "At each free-space point, a starburst of ~20 inward-pointing arrows from all directions (full angular fan)",
    "Draggable active sample point marker (red dot with highlight ring)",
    "Angular distribution readout panel: polar plot of intensity L(θ) versus direction θ around the active point, with radial spokes for sampled directions",
    "Color/intensity coding of rays: rays carrying tree-green, sky-white, ground-black, bird-dark radiance; zero-intensity directions drawn as faint/absent stubs",
    "Coordinate annotation 'L(x, y, θ)' near the active point with live x, y, θ values",
    "Legend: filled arrow = non-zero radiance, dashed/greyed spoke = zero radiance",
    "Annotation callout 'most directions are zero inside the camera' pointing at the interior points"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "movePoint",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "The plenoptic function is defined at every point in space, not just at a camera — moving the sample point continuously changes which scene features each direction sees, so the angular pattern is a property of position."
    },
    {
      "id": "locationView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Sample location",
      "options": [
        "Free space",
        "Inside pinhole camera"
      ],
      "default": "Free space"
    },
    {
      "id": "apertureSize",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Pinhole diameter",
      "range": [
        0.5,
        30,
        0.5
      ],
      "default": 3,
      "teaches": "Widening the pinhole widens the cone of non-zero directions at the interior point: the sparsity of the plenoptic function inside a camera is imposed by the aperture, and a larger aperture trades sharpness for more admitted directions."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "A point in empty space",
      "narration": "Drag the red marker anywhere in the open scene. Every direction around it carries some radiance — green toward the tree, white toward the sky, black toward the ground — so the angular plot is fully populated.",
      "state": {
        "locationView": "Free space",
        "apertureSize": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Position changes the angular pattern",
      "narration": "Move the point closer to the tree and the green wedge of directions grows while the sky wedge shrinks. The same function, evaluated at a different (x, y), gives a different distribution over θ.",
      "state": {
        "locationView": "Free space",
        "apertureSize": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Step inside the pinhole camera",
      "narration": "Now sample a point on the camera's back wall. Almost every spoke goes to zero — only the narrow bundle of directions that passes through the pinhole carries light, and it comes from the opposite side of the scene.",
      "state": {
        "locationView": "Inside pinhole camera",
        "apertureSize": 3
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "Widen the aperture",
      "narration": "Enlarging the pinhole widens the non-zero cone at that same point: more directions light up, and each point now mixes radiance from a broader patch of the scene — the image blurs.",
      "state": {
        "locationView": "Inside pinhole camera",
        "apertureSize": 18
      }
    },
    {
      "title": "A camera is a sparse sample of the plenoptic function",
      "narration": "Shrink the pinhole back down and compare the two readouts side by side: the dense free-space fan versus the single admitted ray. A photograph is just a 2D slice of this much richer function — which is exactly what a NeRF tries to recover.",
      "state": {
        "locationView": "Inside pinhole camera",
        "apertureSize": 0.5
      }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Light exists as an intensity for every direction at every point in space; a camera does not create that function, it merely blocks almost all of it — keeping one ray per image point — which is why recovering the full plenoptic function from a few photographs is the central problem of view synthesis.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape layout, white background, thick black ground bar at the bottom. Right third: cartoon tree with saturated green lobed canopy and brown forked trunk; small black outline bird glyph in the sky above it. Left sixth: tall open black rectangle labelled 'Pinhole camera' in a clean sans serif above it, with a small gap in its right wall as the aperture, and two red dots on its left interior wall. Two long thin black arrows pass through the aperture and cross in an X, each ending at one of the red dots, conveying image inversion. Middle third: two red dots, each surrounded by a radial starburst of about twenty thin inward-pointing arrows spanning all 360 degrees — these are the dense free-space samples. The active sample point is a red dot with a highlight ring and an 'L(x, y, θ)' label showing live values. A side or corner readout panel shows a polar plot of intensity versus direction for the active point: filled colored spokes (green toward tree, light grey/white toward sky, dark toward ground) for non-zero radiance and faint dashed stubs for zero directions. Inside the camera, that polar plot collapses to a single narrow colored wedge with everything else dashed, accompanied by a callout reading 'most directions are zero inside the camera'. Keep line weights thin and uniform, arrowheads small, and use red only for sample points."
}

