userContent.text:
Build an interactive figure using SVG.js.

CONCEPT: The plenoptic function L(x, y, θ) assigns a light intensity to every direction at every point in space; sampling it at a point in free space gives a dense set of rays from all directions, while sampling it inside a pinhole camera gives an almost entirely zero function with energy only along the few directions the aperture admits.
KEY INSIGHT (what students should walk away understanding): Light exists as an intensity for every direction at every point in space; a camera does not create that function, it merely blocks almost all of it — keeping one ray per image point — which is why recovering the full plenoptic function from a few photographs is the central problem of view synthesis.
DEMO STEPS: 5 guided steps — implement the step player in #ui

